course: dictionary
lesson: 12114
title: Colloquial language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Colloquial language is the way people talk with friends and family every day. It is relaxed and informal. Writing for school, letters and exams uses formal language, which is more careful and polite. Both are right, but each belongs in a different place.
explain: Read this chat: «Wanna come to the park? The kids are gonna be there.» The words wanna and gonna are colloquial. Now read this: «Would you like to join us at the park? The children will be there.» That is formal. Same message, different style.
explain: Dictionaries mark colloquial words with a label such as «informal» or «colloquial», so you know to check them. Use them in stories with dialogue, where characters really speak like that. Choose formal words for reports and exam answers.
fact: Colloquial language is informal, everyday speech.
fact: A colloquial word is one used mainly in casual talk, such as «gonna», «telly» or «kids».
fact: «Colloquially» means in an informal, everyday way.
fact: Colloquial English is useful in stories with dialogue, because it makes characters sound like real people.
fact: Formal language is better for school reports and exam answers, where careful, polite words are expected.
exercise: Which sentence is colloquial? | *«Wanna grab a snack after school?» | «I would be delighted to attend.» | «The results were most satisfactory.»
exercise: Which word is colloquial, for television? | broadcast | *telly | programme | transmission
exercise: Which answer is best for a formal exam? | *«The evidence strongly supports this view.» | «Yeah, that's pretty much it, innit.» | «lol, that's well cool.»
test: What does «colloquially» mean in «The word gonna is used colloquially»? | In a foreign language | In a loud and angry way | In a careful, formal way | *In an informal, everyday way
test: Which word would a child use when chatting with friends? | *Kids | Juveniles | Pupils | Offspring
exam: Which phrase is colloquial and should NOT go in a formal exam answer? | *«The results were kinda rubbish.» | «The results were disappointing.» | «The results were not what we hoped.» | «The results were poor.»
